# Copy to .release-request (gitignored) and edit, then: open tools/release.command
#
# FORMAT. This file is `.`-sourced as bash by tools/release.command, after
# .release-env has already set PATH — so it is code, not config, and anything you
# write here executes with the toolchain on PATH. One NAME="value" per line;
# `#` comments and blank lines are fine. LF endings only: a CRLF copy leaves a
# trailing \r inside the last value, and the cut aborts on a component name it
# cannot match. Only COMPONENTS and FLAGS are read — a mistyped FLAG= is not an
# error, it is silently ignored, and the run proceeds as if you had set nothing.
#
# COMPONENTS — one or both of: umbreed umbree
#
#     umbree    the CLI (source: the cli repo's main worktree)
#     umbreed   the home-exit daemon (source: the daemon repo's main worktree)
#
# Cut in order. Each one's marker commit is pushed before the next starts.
# That matters more here than in the sibling channels: this repo's own pre-flight
# refuses to cut while the release repo is ahead of its remote, so an unpushed
# marker does not merely confuse the next component — it ABORTS it.
#
# `all` is not accepted. List the components instead. Unknown names are refused
# before anything is built.
#
# Cut only what CHANGED. A component whose source has not moved since its last
# cut resolves to the same stamp, whose tag already exists, and is refused —
# that is a no-op, not a failure to work around. Drop it from COMPONENTS.
#
# FLAGS — passed to the BUILD half (`rkit build`), which is the half that signs.
# Unlike clawee/burrowee, publishing here is a separate `--distribute-only` step
# the launcher runs for you; it takes no flags from this file. Accepted:
#
#     --public        Developer-ID signing + notarization AND the CVE gate.
#                     The standard ship path. (--public-release is an alias.)
#     --apple         Developer-ID signing + notarization, CVE gate still on —
#                     the gate is ON BY DEFAULT in this repo, which is the
#                     opposite of clawee's --vulncheck opt-in. Do not port that
#                     habit across.
#     --no-vulncheck  skip the CVE gate. Offline rehearsals only.
#     --dry-run       build without bumping the version or needing a real key,
#                     then rehearse the publish half too ("would: ..." only).
#                     Nothing is tagged, uploaded, marked or pushed.
#     --bump-patch    move the patch version before building
#     --bump-minor    move the minor (prompts unless UMBREE_RELEASE_YES=1)
#     --bump-major    move the major (same prompt)
#
# Leaving FLAGS empty is not "defaults", it is a trap: the build still signs with
# the minisign release key and still runs the CVE gate, but it does NOT
# Developer-ID sign or notarize — so it publishes a macOS binary to the PUBLIC
# channel that Gatekeeper will refuse. Say what you want.
#
# VERSIONS. A component's FIRST cut takes NO bump flag — versions/<comp> is
# seeded at the version it should ship (umbreed is seeded 0.1.0). Adding
# --bump-patch to a first cut silently ships 0.1.1 and leaves 0.1.0 never
# released. Every LATER cut needs a bump flag, or the stamp is unchanged and the
# tag already exists.
#
# Republishing an already-staged dist/<stamp>/ is NOT a launcher path:
#
#     bash tools/release.sh --distribute-only <comp> <stamp>
#
# run by hand, with RELEASE_HOST/STATIC_DIR sourced from the sealed configuration.
# --distribute-only is recognised only as release.sh's FIRST argument, because it
# takes its component and stamp positionally — so it cannot be expressed in FLAGS.

COMPONENTS="umbreed"
FLAGS="--public"
